WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.001
Hello. 

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:07.006
[foreign language...] 

00:00:07.006 --> 00:00:12.040
Unless you know Hindi,
you wouldn't understand what I just said. What I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:17.160
said was I wanted to tell you something very&nbsp;
important-- unless you know my language, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:23.360
can't understand it. All of you must know and must&nbsp;
have experienced LLMs (large language models) in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:30.520
the recent times. Large language models are&nbsp;
very popularly known for generating text, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:38.400
it is also important to know that LLMs can also&nbsp;
do a very good job of translating languages. Why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:47.600
is this important? It seems only about 25% of the&nbsp;
Internet users--their primary language is English.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:55.000
And more than 65% of the users on the Internet&nbsp;
prefer to be provided information in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:03.040
primary languages--respective primary languages.&nbsp;
Also, more than 70% of Internet users would like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:12.400
to receive support, issue resolution, etc. in&nbsp;
their preferred languages. Now, because they do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:20.520
not receive the help in their primary languages,&nbsp;
more than 65% of these Internet users are using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:28.000
machine translations to get the help that they&nbsp;
need. So it seems that machine translations are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:36.040
essential for us to do business. So I'm going to&nbsp;
explain machine translations in two parts. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:42.600
I will talk about how we have been doing machine&nbsp;
translation so far, and then I will jump to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:48.880
advantage of the large language models and how we&nbsp;
are going to do translations from them. So let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.560
see how machine translations are done. Machine&nbsp;
translations use artificial intelligence to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:01.760
translate between languages automatically without&nbsp;
any human help. So how do they do that? Let's take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:10.680
an example here: English, Spanish, and Japanese.&nbsp;
To translate between any of these languages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:16.480
you need linguistic rules and you need&nbsp;
dictionaries for each of these languages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:22.947
And the machine translation are done in multiple&nbsp;
approaches. The rule based approach--the rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.947 --> 00:02:28.480
based approach is the one that predominantly&nbsp;
uses the linguistic rules and the dictionaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:34.320
and also the parallel dictionaries that have the&nbsp;
meanings of two different languages, the source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:41.280
language as well as the target language. And then&nbsp;
we have the second approach called the statistical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:47.560
approach. It takes a totally different approach&nbsp;
of leveraging the human translations and learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:53.560
the patterns from them and making very smart&nbsp;
guesses of those translations and delivering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.560 --> 00:03:00.160
those translations. Both approaches work very,&nbsp;
very well by the way. We take it one notch up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:06.560
with the neural approach where, as in rule&nbsp;
based and statistical, it actually is looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:13.000
at each word to get to the translations. Neural&nbsp;
takes it one notch up because it is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:19.720
looking at the sentence constructions to do the&nbsp;
translations. Now, as in any other approach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:27.040
you can take a combination of these approaches and&nbsp;
make it a hybrid approach. So as we discussed, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:34.240
traditional way makes use of the linguistic rules&nbsp;
as well as the dictionaries. And it goes through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:42.480
the supervised learning into one. Large language&nbsp;
models do the translations differently. They make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:49.280
use of the content that is already available in&nbsp;
different languages. We call it the large corpus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:56.520
of parallel text. That is the examples of the same&nbsp;
text in different languages like English, Spanish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:02.840
Japanese and so on. And we feed it to the models.&nbsp;
So the large language models, as you all know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:11.240
use the transformer models and they have both the&nbsp;
encoder and decoder capabilities. On top of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:17.600
the large languages models typically make use of&nbsp;
two approaches in doing the translations. Number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:24.400
one is the sequence-to-sequence approach.&nbsp;
And the sequence-to-sequence approach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:31.800
you can take an input text and feed it to the&nbsp;
encoder. "Hello, how are you?" And the encoder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:38.800
goes through the text and creates the semantic&nbsp;
representation of the text and also captures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:47.080
the meaning of the text and passes it on to the&nbsp;
decoder. Now the decoder is capturing the semantic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:54.520
representation and the meaning and translating&nbsp;
it to the representative target language. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.520 --> 00:05:00.000
say "Hello, how are you?" in English, and if&nbsp;
your target language is going to be Spanish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:07.720
you will get "Hola, cómo estás". The second&nbsp;
approach-- also interesting --is the attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:13.360
model. The attention model is a little bit of a&nbsp;
lazy model compared to the sequence-to-sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:20.200
one. The attention model is focusing on the&nbsp;
main relevant vocabulary of the sentence. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:25.640
is not going through the entire sentence. So&nbsp;
for example, it can pick up "hello" and "how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:33.880
are you" and focus on the "hola" and "cómo&nbsp;
estás". But it is still going to capture the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:40.280
meaning and the semantic representation essence&nbsp;
through the encoded and decoder. As you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:45.240
the larger language models, instead of using&nbsp;
the linguistic rules and the dictionaries are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:52.600
really focusing on capturing the patterns and the&nbsp;
relationships between the data and translating it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:58.520
It's quite obvious now that everybody wants to be&nbsp;
communicated in their own language, including our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:06.000
customers. Let's go leverage large language models&nbsp;
to meet them at the table and communicate in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:18.760
own language. Thank you for watching. Before&nbsp;
you leave, please click subscribe and like.

